.TH CAMERA 3
.SH NAME
camera \- the Raspberry Pi 4's camera receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #V /dev

.B /dev/unicam/ctl
.B /dev/unicam/frame
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The camera device drives Unicam, the receiver on a Raspberry Pi 4's
CSI connector, and nothing beyond it. The power, clocks, lanes, memory
and interrupt are here; what is at the far end of the ribbon, how to
program it, and what to do with its samples are a program's:
.IR camsrv (4)
is that program for the Camera Module 3, and what most people want.
.IR osinit (8)
binds the device after
.BR /dev .
.PP
A frame is CSI-2 RAW10 on two lanes of a non-continuous clock, which the
receiver unpacks into samples of 16 bits.
.PP
Writing to
.B ctl
sets one thing at a time; reading it gives them, with counts of what
has been received.
.PD 0
.TP
.BI format " w h
Frames' size in pixels. The row, at two bytes a pixel, must be a
multiple of 16 bytes. Refused while streaming.
.TP
.B "power on\fR|\fPoff
The receiver's power and the sensor's enable line. A sensor answers on
.IR i2c (3)
after this. Refused while streaming.
.TP
.B "stream on\fR|\fPoff
Receive frames.
.PD
.PP
Each open of
.B frame
waits for the next complete frame and holds it until closed, so a
slow reader reads one whole picture. Reads return its samples, 16 bits
little-endian, row by row. One reader at a time; an open with the
stream off is refused, and one waits a second at most for a frame.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/bcm2711/devcam.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR i2c (3),
.IR camsrv (4),
.IR wm-camera (1)
